WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.510
My name is Maya Gans and I
am so excited to be co-teaching

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.970
the workshop web design
for Shiny developers

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:16.320
this year at posit::conf(2023)
I am David, software developer

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.970
at Novartis

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.520
I want to talk to
you about the workshop

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.320
we are going to give with Maya Gans

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.875
at the Posit conference
at the end of the year.

00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:27.000
There's a lot of reasons
why I think this course is

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.950
important for Shiny developers.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.460
But in this video, I'm going
to distill it to my top three.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.690
The first one is
self-sufficiency in design.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.840
Most applications,
developers work hand in hand

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.010
with designers to
create applications.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.860
But in this course,
we're going to give you

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.110
that autonomy to
feel comfortable

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.440
in your design decisions.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.890
And create an app
all by yourself.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.100
In this workshop, we'll start
from the very beginning,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.170
from the user requirements.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:59.160
You learn how to deal with
them and how to translate them

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.110
into mockups you leverage
wireframing to in the end

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:08.160
come up with a solution
that satisfied the client.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.650
The second skill
is user experience.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.590
So not only will we
define what that means,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.740
but we'll give you the tips
and tricks as well as tools

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.470
to make sure that you're
talking to your end users

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:23.580
and making sure that the app
sparks joy for the people using

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.620
your app on a daily basis.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.420
In this workshop,
you'll learn how

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.120
to deal with these
kind of things.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.690
For instance, you think
of the end users.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.200
What does the end user want
to see when there is an error?

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.980
What does the end
user want to see

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:39.660
when

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.460
there is the computation?

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.130
The last, and what
I think is probably

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.620
the most important skill that
you will get from this course,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.950
is that we're going to
be teaching these design

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:54.960
fundamentals such as color,
typography, layout, using

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.120
the nomenclature and code in
R. So this course is already

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.910
geared to the language
that you know and love,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:06.210
and you will leave
with code ready to go.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.530
I'm convinced by the
end of the workshop,

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:12.570
you will accumulate a lot
of different best practices

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.550
around web design.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.100
So you can quickly
determine what

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.070
is a good UI, what is a bad UI.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:23.220
And the good news doesn't matter
if you are a good R developer,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.260
doesn't matter your
level of Shiny development.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.160
In fact, it's just
about soft skills

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.580
so that you can seamlessly
translate good skills

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.770
into your previous work.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.830
Thanks so much for
watching this video

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.810
and I hope to see you
at the conference.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.040
Thank you so much and
hope to see you there.

